      "problem": "If $g(x)=M\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}$ and $g(4)=3,$ then $M=$",
      "worked_solution": "We substitute $x=4$ and $g(x)=3$ into the expression for $g(x)$ and solve for $M,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =M\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4} \\\\ 3 & =M\\cdot (\\frac{{1}^{4}}{{2}^{4}}) \\\\ 3 & =M\\cdot \\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 3\\cdot 16 & =M \\\\ 48 & =M\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $M=48.$",
